      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 1306378419,
      "text": "Cucumis prophetarum is a dioecious and prostrate or climbing perennial vine in the family Cucurbitaceae. The specific epithet (prophetarum) comes from Latin propheta, meaning \"prophet\"."
    },
    "fr": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Cucumis prophetarum",
      "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cucumis_prophetarum",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 208487521,
      "text": "Cucumis prophetarum est une espèce de plantes à fleurs de la famille des Cucurbitaceae."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "uses": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Cucumis prophetarum § Uses",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cucumis_prophetarum#Uses",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
        "revision": 1306378419,
        "heading": "Uses",
        "text": "The fruit has a bitter flavor when raw and is sometimes boiled or pickled and the leaves are cooked and served with a staple.\nThe fruit is eaten across its native range and occasionally cultivated and sold in local markets.\nThe fruit is also used in folk medicine in Saudi Arabia to treat liver disorders.",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "fr": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Cucumis prophetarum § Utilisations",
        "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cucumis_prophetarum#Utilisations",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
        "revision": 208487521,
        "heading": "Utilisations",
        "text": "Il ne faut la confondre avec d'autres espèces proches ou variétés dont les fruits sont, eux, comestibles.\n\n■ Toxicité\nA l'état naturel, ce fruit contient des cucurbitacines (isocucurbitacine - dihydrocucurbitacine) qui le rend très amer. Ces composés sont toxiques pour les mammifères. Ils sont susceptibles de provoquer des vomissements, coliques et fortes diarrhées.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    },
